A||N||8||Fiero_Freak||sources for 3.4's||||1||
Z||000000||Fiero_Freak||12-09-2000||12:53 PM||||I just needed to know what are the best sources for the 3.4 swap...Preferably one's with the larger valve heads.  Or is it cheaper to get one out of a more none performance oriented car and drop my heads on it?  Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||01:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero_Freak:<BR><B>I just needed to know what are the best sources for the 3.4 swap...Preferably one's with the larger valve heads.  Or is it cheaper to get one out of a more none performance oriented car and drop my heads on it?  Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Dauloverhead cam 3.4 out of aZ34 lumina 93 to 95 is a very good engine to swap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||12-09-2000||01:38 PM||||If you're talking about the iron head (non DOHC) 3.4, get them from any '93-'95 Camaro/Firebird and it will already have the bigger valves (these heads are identical to the Fiero 2.8 heads).<P>You can use a FWD 3.4 block, but not the aluminum heads (or dished pistons)without some fabrication work.<P>If you plan on a total rebuild, then finding a FWD 3.4 shortblock would be the way to go.  Buy new pistons (from iron head 3.4) and install Fiero heads and you're done....the starter holes are already drilled on the "proper" side.<P>The hard part is finding one!  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero_Freak||12-09-2000||01:41 PM||||On the Lumina 3.4, is that using the lumina heads?  Will the DOHC 3.4 accept the fiero intake without mods?  I appreciate the help.  I'll look for both but I think I may have found a Camaro engine.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero_Freak (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||12-09-2000||01:59 PM||||How abotu a 3.4L GM crate Engine?  It will bolt right in and be a fairly easy swap.  My favorite part is that its from GM and not out of a donor car where your not sure how it was driven.  Of course, there is a price penalty for going to GM for the engine, and the starter mounting holes will have to be drilled into it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero_Freak||12-09-2000||11:31 PM||||Thats what I'm trying to avoid at the moment.  I really just want to get it going...and then later rebuild that base the way that I want it.  And hopefully ad O/D.  Any ideas on that?  a four speed auto?||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||12-10-2000||01:11 AM||||The 3.4 DOHC is an involved swap......the Fiero intake will not work with it (only the block is similar to the 2.8).<P>The 3.4 crate motor is the same one used in the '93-'95 Camaro/Firebird.  They are about $1600-$1800 and don't include the fuel injectors (although some people have successfully used the 2.8 injectors).<P>You can install the 4 sp. auto, but you'll have to shorten one or both of the axles (not sure).  You'll also need to find the proper tranny mounts (stock Fiero mounts will not work).<P>There are several low mileage Camaro 3.4's here in Denver.  They run anywhere from $600-$900 with 15-50K miles.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bubbajoexx||12-10-2000||12:34 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero_Freak:<BR><B>Thats what I'm trying to avoid at the moment.  I really just want to get it going...and then later rebuild that base the way that I want it.  And hopefully ad O/D.  Any ideas on that?  a four speed auto?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>yes use the thd 440 trans axle no computer need to control the shifts they are plentiful and cheep. and use the mount from a pontiac 6000 with this trans axle the fit the fiero  cradle fin no mods stock fiero automatic axles fit this trans.<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||tgowens||12-11-2000||12:37 AM||||Fiero_Freak, mrfiero is leading you the right way - the 3.4 is a fairly easy, minor modification swap with more horses and considerably more torque. From the heads up it is just about as simple as "remove from the 2.8 and bolt on to the 3.4". We do 2.5L to 3.4L and 2.8L to 3.4L swaps all the time and other than drilling the three starter holes on the other side of the block there are no more major mods........bolts up to whatever tranny you have in there.......any of the other engines out there (3.1, 3.8 etc are going to be much more complicated......the only other one that is fairly easy is the 4.9L -- major mod there is the front motor mount and basically it is a bolt up to an automatic only.......<P>Anyway - mrfiero has the right info there for ya!||||reg||1
